1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,840
Hello, this is Yamyam Coding.

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:06,360
AI has begun writing code and creating game
scenes.

3
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:12,320
What value will our years of programming,
drawing and game-design experience have?

4
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:17,380
We will trace how development tools have
changed, consider automation and work,

5
00:00:17,780 --> 00:00:22,160
then use an actual shop system to see where a
developer’s judgment matters.

6
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:26,360
First, let us look at the work we have
delegated to development tools.

7
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:32,380
As tools handle more complexity, how does that
change the choices and judgments people make?

8
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:37,220
Epic Games’ Unreal Engine is a useful example
of that change.

9
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:42,360
Unreal has long developed visual tools that
people can work with comfortably.

10
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:45,240
Kismet and Blueprints are examples.

11
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:52,220
In Blueprints, you arrange nodes representing
functions and connect them to define behavior:

12
00:00:52,220 --> 00:00:57,980
when a door opens, how a character attacks, or
what happens in response to an event.

13
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:01,040
A major advantage is that designers unfamiliar
with

14
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:04,060
writing text code can build game behavior
themselves.

15
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:08,040
Visual workflows let people inspect behavior
and its

16
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:11,660
overall flow without focusing only on the
details of code.

17
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:15,380
Nanite and Lumen can be considered in a
similar context.

18
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:21,320
As the engine takes on more work behind
complex scenes,

19
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:25,720
creators can concentrate on composing scenes
and evaluating the results.

20
00:01:25,780 --> 00:01:31,120
Enabling a feature does not automatically
solve performance and quality

21
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:34,240
problems in every project. Still, tools have
increasingly taken over complex,

22
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:39,680
repetitive work while people compose scenes
and evaluate them.

23
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:43,700
Until now, people have operated these tools
directly.

24
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:49,340
Delegating work to AI introduces another
requirement.

25
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:52,100
An interface that people find easy to see and
manipulate may

26
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:56,360
differ from one that lets AI read and modify a
project effectively.

27
00:01:56,720 --> 00:02:00,520
People can understand a structure from the
placement of nodes and the flow of

28
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:05,520
connections. To delegate work to AI, we must
clearly expose available functions,

29
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:09,260
permitted changes and ways to check the
result.

30
00:02:09,260 --> 00:02:14,920
Alongside a human-friendly editor, we need
ways for AI to call functions and

31
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:19,700
inspect their outcomes. This is entering the
design of next-generation engines.

32
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:26,980
Epic’s announced UE6 plans include Verse, a
new gameplay architecture and connections

33
00:02:26,980 --> 00:02:33,700
between AI tools and engine functionality
through MCP. Blueprints and Actors are

34
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:38,920
planned to remain in early UE6, then be
replaced gradually with migration tools as

35
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:43,340
the new architecture matures. This does not
mean Blueprints disappear immediately.

36
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:46,640
Here, think of MCP as a protocol connecting AI
to

37
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:49,620
information and functions provided by external
tools.

38
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:55,080
Instead of merely answering development
questions,

39
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:59,980
an AI workflow can inspect a project, execute
an available function and check what happened.

40
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:04,900
The change therefore goes beyond putting a
chat window beside the developer.

41
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:10,100
It involves adapting the engine and
development process for working with AI.

42
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:15,880
Separate from that long-term engine plan,
reports are also describing changes

43
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:20,200
within production teams. Bloomberg recently
reported that Epic’s Disney

44
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:24,440
collaboration was delayed and developers were
asked to expand their use of AI.

45
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:28,480
If this change continues, writing every line
of code by hand may no

46
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:31,900
longer be the starting assumption for every
development process.

47
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:34,780
The pace and extent of change will vary by
company.

48
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:39,620
But it is worth considering how expectations
of developers may change.

49
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:45,440
Alongside the ability to implement a feature
directly, the ability to use tools, including

50
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:51,260
AI, to build accurately, verify and finish a
product may become increasingly important.

51
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:55,720
Game developers have already experienced
changes in the skills

52
00:03:56,120 --> 00:03:59,320
needed when tools take over work once
performed directly.

53
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:01,560
Consider how tools such as Substance Painter,

54
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:05,840
Photoshop and ZBrush changed the way people
work.

55
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:11,600
A texture is an image applied to a model’s
surface. UV coordinates determine

56
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:16,880
which part of the image belongs on which face.
Think of an unfolded paper box.

57
00:04:17,160 --> 00:04:22,440
Earlier workflows required painting color and
detail while looking at flattened UV layouts.

58
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,860
Newer tools made it common to paint directly
on a model,

59
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:31,360
work with several material properties together
and automate repetitive work.

60
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:34,760
Transferring details from a high-resolution
model

61
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:37,180
to another model also became part of
production.

62
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:40,520
When tools change, the route to the same
result changes,

63
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:43,540
along with the expertise expected in a
workplace.

64
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:46,740
Traditional methods can still be sufficient in
some situations.

65
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:51,940
But participating in a new production process
requires learning its tools and workflows.

66
00:04:52,380 --> 00:04:56,440
This does not make the time spent learning
older skills meaningless.

67
00:04:56,800 --> 00:05:02,760
Understanding form, distinguishing materials
and judging quality still matter.

68
00:05:03,220 --> 00:05:06,020
What changes is how that understanding becomes
a result.

69
00:05:06,360 --> 00:05:12,260
AI can also be considered as part of this
change in tools. But another question remains:

70
00:05:12,260 --> 00:05:18,980
the continuing value of judgment does not mean
every existing job will remain unchanged.

71
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:22,340
Manufacturing, which experienced automation
before game development,

72
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:24,880
makes that distinction clearer.

73
00:05:25,160 --> 00:05:28,500
Imagine robots and automated machinery
entering a factory

74
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:32,000
process where people moved parts and repeated
the same operations.

75
00:05:32,340 --> 00:05:37,960
When machines take over repetitive tasks, some
people escape difficult,

76
00:05:38,220 --> 00:05:41,800
monotonous work. The change can be positive if
they can move into another role.

77
00:05:42,020 --> 00:05:44,960
But not everyone finds another job.

78
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:50,120
Sometimes the change goes beyond making a task
easier and reduces the number of people

79
00:05:50,620 --> 00:05:55,160
employed to do it. Productivity gains and
individual employment insecurity can coexist.

80
00:05:55,660 --> 00:05:59,240
To understand automation’s impact, we must
consider both what

81
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:03,660
becomes easier and whose work shrinks or
requires a different role.

82
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:07,640
Does a shrinking share of jobs necessarily
mean the

83
00:06:07,960 --> 00:06:11,500
skills learned through that work lose all
their value?

84
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:13,780
Handmade clothing and furniture help us
distinguish

85
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,220
changes in employment from the value of a
craft.

86
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:19,620
Even after mass production became widespread,

87
00:06:20,020 --> 00:06:24,640
some people continued seeking handmade
furniture and carefully carved ornament.

88
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:30,520
Such objects are not judged only by their
function. Materials, workmanship,

89
00:06:30,520 --> 00:06:36,520
attention to finish and the identity of their
maker can contribute to their value.

90
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:40,280
Of course, handmade does not automatically
mean better.

91
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:45,400
Distinctive quality and expression must
accompany the fact that a person made it.

92
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:50,920
Making an object that serves a function
differs from giving it a distinctive value.

93
00:06:51,100 --> 00:06:55,740
An easier process does not make every choice
of materials and expression identical.

94
00:06:56,160 --> 00:07:01,560
We can consider the same distinction in
digital work.

95
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:06,640
As producing an output gets cheaper, its mere
existence may be less exceptional.

96
00:07:06,900 --> 00:07:11,600
People may instead look more closely at the
choices and intentions behind it.

97
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:17,240
Similar kinds of pictures can feel different
depending on their composition,

98
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:20,400
intended atmosphere and expression.

99
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:22,120
Games are no different.

100
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:28,180
There is a gap between a game running and a
player having a distinctive experience.

101
00:07:28,580 --> 00:07:33,440
Even when the same character receives the same
potion,

102
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:37,140
changing a number feels different from
combining a character reaction,

103
00:07:37,620 --> 00:07:41,220
sound and reward display. Choosing what to
show and when changes the experience.

104
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:47,600
Some people wanted to test those choices but
were held back by programming.

105
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:51,660
That is one opportunity AI creation tools
could expand.

106
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:55,780
Unity Spark illustrates this direction.

107
00:07:56,300 --> 00:08:00,860
Unity describes Spark as a service for
creating and sharing games in a browser

108
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:04,660
without prior Unity or game-development
experience. As checked on October14,2026,

109
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:11,000
its official page says it is coming soon and
offers a waitlist.

110
00:08:11,060 --> 00:08:15,480
Unity also provides AI tools in its existing
development environment

111
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:19,640
for answering questions, writing code and
performing editor tasks.

112
00:08:19,980 --> 00:08:23,420
This can be seen as widening the entrance to
creation,

113
00:08:23,420 --> 00:08:26,740
much as Roblox lets people who enjoy games
participate in making them.

114
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:30,260
Some experienced players can explain

115
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:33,100
precisely what their favorite genre is
missing.

116
00:08:33,420 --> 00:08:36,000
Some artists have a world they want to create
but

117
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,060
have been unable to start because of
programming.

118
00:08:39,060 --> 00:08:42,580
AI-assisted tools could give these people more

119
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:45,220
opportunities to try their ideas in a working
form.

120
00:08:45,680 --> 00:08:47,980
However, being able to start a game and

121
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:51,120
finishing a polished game are different
things.

122
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:56,960
A wider entrance can be welcome. Yet the
process that someone can now begin

123
00:08:56,960 --> 00:09:00,240
easily may also be work another person has
spent years learning and loving.

124
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:03,420
For those people, changes in the creative
process

125
00:09:03,420 --> 00:09:06,140
can bring a sense of loss alongside
opportunity.

126
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:10,800
Indie developer Thomas Brush's concerns offer
an example of this feeling.

127
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:13,460
Thomas Brush is a developer behind games
including Pinstripe,

128
00:09:13,460 --> 00:09:15,760
Neversong and Twisted Tower.

129
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,980
He has expressed sadness that the coding,
animation and digital drawing

130
00:09:21,340 --> 00:09:26,060
he practiced from childhood seem to be losing
their place in the industry.

131
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:28,140
That feeling is understandable.

132
00:09:28,140 --> 00:09:32,100
We do not always learn an activity solely
because we like its output.

133
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:34,660
We may learn programming because we love
writing code,

134
00:09:34,660 --> 00:09:39,160
fixing mistakes and finally seeing an idea
behave as intended.

135
00:09:39,540 --> 00:09:42,000
We may love drawing because of the process of

136
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,180
building an image through lines and layers of
color.

137
00:09:44,580 --> 00:09:49,020
The process itself was a source of joy and
part of an identity.

138
00:09:49,440 --> 00:09:52,880
This feeling is not limited to one developer.

139
00:09:53,180 --> 00:09:58,320
Think about why making something and seeing it
work can stay with us for years.

140
00:09:58,540 --> 00:10:03,140
Think of writing a short BASIC program, moving
a drawing with ActionScript in Flash,

141
00:10:03,280 --> 00:10:09,840
or modifying a game mod. Each small change on
screen becomes a way to learn how it works.

142
00:10:10,140 --> 00:10:14,000
Making music or filming video offers a similar
experience: change a sound

143
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:18,380
or composition and observe the result. With
automation and robotics,

144
00:10:18,380 --> 00:10:23,640
those changes become the movements of real
machines.

145
00:10:24,220 --> 00:10:28,760
These activities share something: using a
principle you understand to make something,

146
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:32,540
then seeing it move or hearing it produce
sound.

147
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:34,900
After accumulating those experiences,

148
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:41,020
it is natural to have complicated feelings
when the process changes dramatically.

149
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:45,840
That sense of loss is understandable. Still, a
change in familiar

150
00:10:45,840 --> 00:10:49,440
methods and the disappearance of human skill
are different things.

151
00:10:49,680 --> 00:10:55,060
People still forge objects from metal and make
furniture by hand.

152
00:10:55,320 --> 00:10:59,300
A craft’s share of industrial work and its
working methods can

153
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:04,380
change without eliminating the skill or
everything it can express.

154
00:11:04,660 --> 00:11:06,760
We can see a similar change in painting.

155
00:11:07,240 --> 00:11:11,120
Painting continues despite photography and
digital tools.

156
00:11:11,440 --> 00:11:16,440
People still admire particular artists’
expression and seek their work.

157
00:11:16,680 --> 00:11:22,240
Bringing this perspective to digital work
raises the question of where its value lies.

158
00:11:22,620 --> 00:11:26,200
How can digital works retain value over time?
Attempts such as NFTs changed approaches

159
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:34,220
to ownership and trading, but a digital format
alone does not determine a work's value.

160
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:38,160
Games offer a special case for that question
because we can

161
00:11:38,440 --> 00:11:44,960
directly experience what a digital creation
gives a player.

162
00:11:45,180 --> 00:11:47,580
Alongside art, music and story,

163
00:11:47,580 --> 00:11:50,480
games contain structures that respond to the
player’s actions.

164
00:11:50,760 --> 00:11:56,700
We do not simply watch. We move, choose, fail
and try again.

165
00:11:57,100 --> 00:12:00,540
Many different skills are connected into one
experience.

166
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:04,180
When one person or a small team completes such
an experience,

167
00:12:04,180 --> 00:12:09,120
we should consider what they created as well
as which tools they used.

168
00:12:09,340 --> 00:12:12,440
If AI tools expand what a small team can
express,

169
00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:17,100
experiences that once seemed out of reach may
become finished works.

170
00:12:17,340 --> 00:12:20,580
At the same time, we need to consider how
using these

171
00:12:20,580 --> 00:12:23,900
tools affects other people's work and
employment.

172
00:12:24,320 --> 00:12:29,480
To think about that impact, we can return to
automation in manufacturing.

173
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:33,600
Cars and mass-produced furniture are everyday
examples.

174
00:12:33,900 --> 00:12:37,060
Machines are deeply involved in producing many
goods.

175
00:12:37,600 --> 00:12:43,960
Consumers do not always examine whether a
person directly performed every step.

176
00:12:44,300 --> 00:12:47,880
As machinery handles processing and assembly,

177
00:12:47,880 --> 00:12:51,280
people's roles may shift toward monitoring
equipment and intervening when problems arise.

178
00:12:51,280 --> 00:12:54,680
The range of work that can be automated now
extends beyond

179
00:12:54,680 --> 00:12:57,740
physical labor to intellectual tasks such as
coding and design.

180
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:00,980
This comparison helps explain the broader
direction of automation.

181
00:13:01,560 --> 00:13:06,020
But the existence of past automation does not
answer every present-day question.

182
00:13:06,980 --> 00:13:11,900
The conditions under which creators’ work is
used, the rights in outputs

183
00:13:11,900 --> 00:13:15,940
and how the burden of change falls on workers
must each be considered.

184
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:22,000
We must consider both what a tool can do and
appropriate ways to use it.

185
00:13:22,420 --> 00:13:25,540
Taking responsibility for an outcome includes
these judgments.

186
00:13:26,240 --> 00:13:28,640
In development, that responsibility reveals
the difference between getting

187
00:13:28,640 --> 00:13:34,740
a feature to work once and completing a
product people can continue using.

188
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:38,200
Think of making something with AI. When the
requested feature first works,

189
00:13:38,300 --> 00:13:42,400
it can feel as though the product is almost
finished.

190
00:13:42,640 --> 00:13:46,760
AI may make it easier to produce a first
version of software.

191
00:13:47,040 --> 00:13:51,020
But a working result and a finished product
are different stages.

192
00:13:51,300 --> 00:13:53,060
The important word here is product.

193
00:13:53,060 --> 00:13:58,180
Suppose a button appears, a character moves
and an enemy attacks.

194
00:13:58,740 --> 00:14:00,640
Imagine a game where players paint the ground.

195
00:14:01,020 --> 00:14:05,920
Getting a character to move and leave paint
demonstrates an important feature. That

196
00:14:06,340 --> 00:14:11,960
screen alone does not establish multiplayer
behavior or the quality of an entire match.

197
00:14:12,220 --> 00:14:15,940
Confirming movement and painting is a
meaningful first step.

198
00:14:16,340 --> 00:14:18,920
But a product people keep using must handle

199
00:14:18,920 --> 00:14:22,220
conditions absent from that first
demonstration.

200
00:14:22,880 --> 00:14:25,840
Let us return to the potion shop. This time,
we will examine the

201
00:14:26,040 --> 00:14:31,240
conditions governing a purchase rather than
how its reward is presented.

202
00:14:31,520 --> 00:14:35,920
Suppose we asked AI to implement buying items
with gold.

203
00:14:36,140 --> 00:14:40,960
Pressing the button subtracts gold and adds an
item. One attempt seems to work.

204
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:48,100
The character has100 gold and a potion
costs30. Buying one should leave70 gold and

205
00:14:48,400 --> 00:14:55,020
add one potion to the inventory. Both the
charge and the item grant must be correct.

206
00:14:55,260 --> 00:15:00,020
What if gold is insufficient? What if the
inventory is full?

207
00:15:00,560 --> 00:15:04,020
Could repeated button presses process a
purchase twice?

208
00:15:04,240 --> 00:15:09,540
Now set the balance to20. The same button
should reject the purchase.

209
00:15:09,800 --> 00:15:13,340
The balance must remain20 and no potion should
be added.

210
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:18,680
We must also define what happens when the bag
is full. Here, we reject the purchase and

211
00:15:18,680 --> 00:15:23,900
leave the gold unchanged. The player must not
lose money without receiving an item.

212
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:29,380
After checking money and capacity, we must
inspect the request itself.

213
00:15:29,840 --> 00:15:35,320
Processing the same request twice is one
example. A player asking to buy twice

214
00:15:35,620 --> 00:15:39,700
differs from one request arriving twice
because of a communication problem.

215
00:15:40,040 --> 00:15:44,440
The latter requires a way to recognize and
process the request only once.

216
00:15:44,500 --> 00:15:48,080
In multiplayer, we must consider differences
between players’ states.

217
00:15:48,200 --> 00:15:51,300
Results should also survive saving and
restarting.

218
00:15:51,460 --> 00:15:55,920
A check mark on screen does not verify
persistence or multiplayer.

219
00:15:55,920 --> 00:16:01,200
We must reconnect, compare states and examine
failures.

220
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:05,400
Who defines these conditions and decides what
to check?

221
00:16:05,620 --> 00:16:07,840
This is where a developer’s understanding
matters.

222
00:16:08,100 --> 00:16:10,060
Faster code generation does not automatically
clarify

223
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:13,900
a system’s requirements and responsibilities.

224
00:16:14,220 --> 00:16:17,520
That is why understanding requirements and
designing

225
00:16:17,520 --> 00:16:20,560
how conditions interact in a complex system
matter.

226
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:27,620
The more we delegate to AI, the more clearly
we must describe the result and its

227
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:32,200
evaluation criteria. We can now consider how
to put those criteria into an AI workflow.

228
00:16:32,880 --> 00:16:35,760
We can turn the shop’s purchase conditions
into requirements

229
00:16:36,100 --> 00:16:41,040
and connect stages that implement and check
them.

230
00:16:41,400 --> 00:16:45,780
An AI can read requirements, change code,

231
00:16:46,220 --> 00:16:48,960
run tests and revise failures.

232
00:16:49,140 --> 00:16:55,040
The process may need information from previous
work,

233
00:16:55,420 --> 00:16:58,700
evaluation of the current result and
verification through actual execution.

234
00:16:58,700 --> 00:17:02,920
Yet connecting many stages does not
automatically produce a good product.

235
00:17:03,260 --> 00:17:07,560
If implementation follows an incorrect
requirement and verification

236
00:17:07,800 --> 00:17:10,860
repeats that same assumption, a wrong result
can pass several stages.

237
00:17:11,340 --> 00:17:14,660
If duplicate requests never appear in the shop
requirements,

238
00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:19,020
a test that only checks one successful
purchase may miss the problem.

239
00:17:19,360 --> 00:17:21,920
Look beyond a passing result to what was
tested.

240
00:17:21,920 --> 00:17:26,880
Checking normal purchases, insufficient funds,

241
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:31,780
inventory capacity and repeated delivery of
one request can expose gaps.

242
00:17:32,100 --> 00:17:34,300
People who understand development from
beginning

243
00:17:34,300 --> 00:17:36,340
to end therefore still have an important role.

244
00:17:36,600 --> 00:17:40,180
They can notice missing conditions,

245
00:17:40,180 --> 00:17:44,080
decide where to look and anticipate
interactions between features.

246
00:17:44,220 --> 00:17:49,980
Even after all these checks pass, one question
remains: is the game fun?

247
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:54,920
Experience gained through playing games can
help answer that question.

248
00:17:55,180 --> 00:17:58,260
Someone who has compared many games over time
may recognize

249
00:17:58,320 --> 00:18:01,160
what creates enjoyment and what interrupts its
flow.

250
00:18:01,520 --> 00:18:05,520
They may explain which rewards feel
satisfying,

251
00:18:05,580 --> 00:18:08,700
when combat gets boring or what feels
unresponsive.

252
00:18:09,040 --> 00:18:12,640
That judgment can help when making games with
AI.

253
00:18:12,940 --> 00:18:18,580
Even two jumps covering the same distance can
feel different if one responds late.

254
00:18:18,580 --> 00:18:21,540
Adjusting the response and playing again turns
a

255
00:18:21,540 --> 00:18:25,180
vague feeling into a concrete condition we can
change.

256
00:18:25,580 --> 00:18:28,740
However, explaining fun and implementing it

257
00:18:28,740 --> 00:18:31,940
reliably are abilities that should develop
together.

258
00:18:32,420 --> 00:18:37,720
An appealing feature does not automatically
solve persistence or performance.

259
00:18:38,020 --> 00:18:42,460
Technical stability alone does not make a game
fun either.

260
00:18:42,880 --> 00:18:46,940
Advances in AI tools may also help narrow the
gap

261
00:18:47,480 --> 00:18:49,920
between a sense of fun and a reliable
implementation.

262
00:18:50,160 --> 00:18:53,960
If AI improves at coordinating work and
verifying results,

263
00:18:54,380 --> 00:18:58,340
it may take on a larger role in building more
complex products.

264
00:18:58,540 --> 00:19:01,000
That is a forecast, not a claim that the
process is solved for every product today.

265
00:19:01,260 --> 00:19:06,760
Even if we delegate more implementation,

266
00:19:07,140 --> 00:19:10,460
choosing the experience we want to create
remains important.

267
00:19:10,880 --> 00:19:14,860
To consider the value of that choice,

268
00:19:14,860 --> 00:19:18,240
think of a game remembered for something other
than how closely it resembles reality.

269
00:19:18,520 --> 00:19:20,740
World of Warcraft is one such example.

270
00:19:21,040 --> 00:19:24,240
Its longstanding art direction,

271
00:19:24,240 --> 00:19:26,560
sound and atmosphere work together to create a
distinctive impression.

272
00:19:26,900 --> 00:19:29,500
Images that look like direct copies of reality

273
00:19:29,500 --> 00:19:31,980
are not the only ones people can love for
years.

274
00:19:32,260 --> 00:19:34,880
Exaggerated shapes, painted-looking textures,

275
00:19:34,880 --> 00:19:38,900
colors and music suited to a world can combine
into a distinctive impression.

276
00:19:38,900 --> 00:19:42,140
The point is not to judge a work solely by
whether

277
00:19:42,140 --> 00:19:45,460
it uses a particular rendering technique.

278
00:19:45,740 --> 00:19:50,940
It is to consider the visual choices and how
well they fit the game’s experience.

279
00:19:51,360 --> 00:19:54,400
Therefore, the ability to create more
realistic images

280
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:57,480
does not mean every game must pursue the same
direction.

281
00:19:57,760 --> 00:20:02,820
The comparison on screen changes the
presentation of the same character

282
00:20:03,280 --> 00:20:05,080
in the same place. Instead of declaring one
universally better,

283
00:20:05,080 --> 00:20:09,840
we should ask which fits our intended world
and experience.

284
00:20:10,100 --> 00:20:12,660
Some games impress through their resemblance
to reality.

285
00:20:12,660 --> 00:20:18,000
Others remain memorable because they depict
worlds in impossible ways.

286
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,980
A consistent choice of expression distinct
from

287
00:20:20,980 --> 00:20:24,140
reality can help preserve a work's character
over time.

288
00:20:24,580 --> 00:20:27,040
As AI makes output easier to produce,

289
00:20:27,040 --> 00:20:31,640
these distinctive choices may become even more
important in telling works apart.

290
00:20:32,480 --> 00:20:34,620
Remembering that individuality can lead us to
the

291
00:20:34,620 --> 00:20:39,280
names and faces of the people who made those
choices.

292
00:20:39,580 --> 00:20:43,080
Think of the music and art of Halo and The
Elder Scrolls.

293
00:20:43,360 --> 00:20:47,960
Familiar sounds and atmospheres remind us of
the sensibilities of their creators.

294
00:20:48,260 --> 00:20:54,240
When we love a work, we may want more than a
similar output from just anyone.

295
00:20:54,500 --> 00:20:58,240
We become curious about what its creator will
show us next.

296
00:20:58,240 --> 00:21:04,020
We look forward to their choice of scenes,
sounds and experiences.

297
00:21:04,240 --> 00:21:08,260
That trust does not arise simply from
producing more outputs.

298
00:21:08,440 --> 00:21:13,460
It grows through consistent choices and
responsibility for their results.

299
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:19,840
The skills built through hands-on creation
contribute to that judgment. Even as the

300
00:21:19,840 --> 00:21:24,360
process changes, experience can guide how we
select and refine the output of new tools.

301
00:21:24,620 --> 00:21:29,820
Skills you developed directly can remain a
basis for evaluating new tools.

302
00:21:30,300 --> 00:21:34,420
You can explain why a form in an AI-generated
image looks awkward.

303
00:21:34,580 --> 00:21:37,040
You can read generated code and identify a
problem

304
00:21:37,040 --> 00:21:39,860
that may emerge later even though it works
now.

305
00:21:39,860 --> 00:21:45,540
You can judge what to remove from a generated
scene so its intended emotion becomes clearer.

306
00:21:45,840 --> 00:21:50,020
The shop illustrates this too. Finding a
missing purchase condition and

307
00:21:50,020 --> 00:21:55,620
improving the timing of a reward call on
different experiences and judgments.

308
00:21:56,040 --> 00:21:58,480
Even when directing several AI tools,

309
00:21:58,480 --> 00:22:03,180
your choices about what to accept and what to
change will show in the outcome.

310
00:22:03,600 --> 00:22:07,400
The relationship resembles a tool and its
user's skill. Even when a tool provides

311
00:22:07,860 --> 00:22:13,220
more power, the user must decide where and how
much of it the work requires.

312
00:22:13,400 --> 00:22:18,140
A power tool can speed up work. But without
knowing the force required,

313
00:22:18,140 --> 00:22:23,620
the material and when to stop, you can damage
a screw or a component.

314
00:22:23,920 --> 00:22:27,600
A tool’s increased power does not replace its
user’s understanding.

315
00:22:27,800 --> 00:22:32,220
A skilled person understands what a tool can
do and adjusts its power to the task.

316
00:22:32,220 --> 00:22:34,720
A similar change can occur in development
tools.

317
00:22:35,060 --> 00:22:41,260
Delegating parts of previously manual work
lets a person handle a wider scope.

318
00:22:41,740 --> 00:22:44,840
That makes it important to judge what to
delegate, which results to

319
00:22:45,140 --> 00:22:48,140
accept and where to intervene. When many
people use the same tools,

320
00:22:48,580 --> 00:22:53,740
some problems also extend beyond individual
expertise.

321
00:22:54,240 --> 00:22:57,060
This is about shared standards and
responsibility.

322
00:22:57,260 --> 00:23:02,060
Consider how roads and traffic rules changed
as cars became widespread.

323
00:23:02,240 --> 00:23:05,440
New technology changes more than its operating
instructions.

324
00:23:05,620 --> 00:23:07,580
Accidents occur, responsibility must be
assigned

325
00:23:07,580 --> 00:23:10,520
and standards are needed so people can use it
together.

326
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:15,960
AI-assisted development and creation will
involve adjustments to working practices,

327
00:23:15,960 --> 00:23:18,100
responsibility, rights and expectations
alongside technical performance.

328
00:23:18,300 --> 00:23:20,200
We are in the middle of that change.

329
00:23:20,200 --> 00:23:27,380
We do not yet know precisely which tasks will
be automated or which roles will

330
00:23:27,640 --> 00:23:31,660
shrink or grow. But even amid uncertainty, we
can use the knowledge we already have.

331
00:23:32,080 --> 00:23:34,840
Rather than immediately treating existing
skills as worthless,

332
00:23:35,200 --> 00:23:38,700
use them to judge what new tools produce.

333
00:23:39,060 --> 00:23:42,080
Apply your knowledge, use the tools and

334
00:23:42,460 --> 00:23:46,460
learn how far you can responsibly delegate.

335
00:23:46,800 --> 00:23:50,880
People who understand code can see more in
generated code. People who understand

336
00:23:51,160 --> 00:23:56,380
art can identify specific problems and
possibilities in generated images.

337
00:23:56,600 --> 00:24:01,160
People who understand games can consider how a
working feature becomes an experience.

338
00:24:01,160 --> 00:24:04,180
As tools advance, the process of making things
will change.

339
00:24:04,460 --> 00:24:08,320
Our accumulated skills can find new uses
within it.

340
00:24:08,580 --> 00:24:11,340
When using AI, which tasks became easier,

341
00:24:11,340 --> 00:24:15,240
and where did you find your own knowledge more
necessary?

342
00:24:15,460 --> 00:24:16,920
Share your experience in the comments.

343
00:24:17,160 --> 00:24:19,880
This was Yamyam Coding. Thank you.
